ample  would be the report we did on testing  tracing and isolation another one on the  use of face masks as other reports in in  process so trying to sort of look at the  different approaches disciplines take to  evidence what kind of evidence they  value let me give you an example I don't  think in a pandemic like this you can  put too much weight on randomized  control trials not because they're not  useful when you can have them but many  of the pieces of advice you're giving  simply don't have an RCT evidence base  so if you said well we're only going to  do those things that are proven by  enemy's control trials we're certainly  not going to get very far when the  degree of uncertainty and lack of  appropriate knowledge exists so it's  kind of then working together and saying  well what evidence is persuasive and and  also getting a wider a wider perspective  on on on the evidence to what extent we  want to bring the economic cost in  alongside some of the the costs to  infection you know there's been this  balancing act which I'm sure you're  familiar with in Canada between trying  to get the public health right and  trying to get the economics right  without without compromising either too  much and and again through conversations  within within a multidisciplinary  environment we can we can sort of start  to speak each other's language or  understand each other's language but  ultimately the view is we need to put  out there the best of thinking that we  can find and and then input that into  the policy process yeah it's it's very  helpful I think I think the interesting  approach to to look out to different  jurisdictions so I often think about  this is one of the things that CFR finds  really valuable in our process is having  research programs that are made up of  senior academic leaders who are based in  different institutions and different  disciplines and in different countries  being able to bring that together we  were able to hold an international 