k a little bit about  the role that you see of the firms and  the private sector as part of the  pandemic response so we think about  public health and the  cows response we think about economic  stimulus and innovation as a form of  recovery out of the pandemic but what  have you seen in terms of the dynamics  around the private sector engaging with  the government or being part of the  public health response there's a couple  of things on that I'd like to say what  one is the sort of direct involvement in  the private sector trying to deliver a  kind of recovery phase there is little  doubt that we will have to get the  private sector back into some kind of  decent shape to provide the jobs and  livelihoods for people who've been  affected by the pandemic but equally  there will be a different world out  there one where business as usual will  not be possible in many sectors of the  economy at least for the foreseeable  future - we hopefully get a vaccine or a  more effective treatment and so it's a  case of trying to work with with  business to to make the best of what is  a difficult situation for example what  kind of testing protocols does one want  in place how does one encourage them are  they a matter of voluntary compliance or  compulsion I think we all think that in  the end trying to get people to do  things voluntarily is advantageous but  that's not always feasible so you have  to decide where there's the limelight  regulation and volunteerism  but many firms that particularly those  that want to get back to normal business  have stepped up and started voluntarily  to engage in public health measures and  not an entirely frivolous example is the  professional soccer to you football to  us leagues which have basically in order  to be able to be allowed to play again  have put in very rigorous testing  regimes for players and staff because  they realized that was the only route  back to some kind of normal operation of  their business even though in fact