ectly to a  scientist and say this is one for the  scientist now please answer them there's  no the scientists can't hide at that  point because they either state an  opinion or the  so then you can't just say a scientist  believes you really you know figure out  who's who's saying what but I think  there's a sort of wider background  concern and this is why it's been  evolving through the pandemic because  initially the main scientific body in  the UK Sage there was not even any  transparency about who sat on that body  and then at one point they were forced  to publish the names and and as time  goes by people are asking for even more  information maybe they should publish  the minutes for those of us who used to  work in central banking will be familiar  with this I used to publish our central  bank minutes after every policy decision  to communicate to the public why we were  reaching a decision but now now that may  become a feature of these more  institutionalized scientific bodies that  they're they have to publish minutes and  then maybe they also have to publish  some of the sources for that those  minutes which particular studies because  as we know it's rarely that you have  just a single study that's going to  convince you to shift policy but if  that's really what shifter policy people  should be honest about that what was the  evidence that was decisive and so this  is a this has been a very interesting  area to watch evolving countries will  learn from each other they'll look at  what systems are in place and decide  whether they want the more the system in  which the politicians have put more on  the pedestal less on the pedestal quoted  not quoted but but I think because this  is all hitters in such a short time  we're very much learning on on the job  as far as that goes  absolutely so really interesting  question around the politicization of  science of bodies so we've seen for  example in the u.s. around face masks  and face masks where in that t