ily resolve the  issue because the issue is itself very  complex you can by Framing in a  particular way make transparent to those  who have to make decisions kind of what  the pros and cons are broadly of taking  different stances and where the evidence  would definitely say you shouldn't do  something versus where it doesn't prove  you shouldn't do something but it might  be prudent to do it so I think one of  the things that I've always found  rewarding if you like engagement in  policy is where you think you can you  can make a contribution by framing an  issue in a in a in a particular in a  particular way so so I think that's  that's a valuable part of what we do and  link to that academics can take a long  term view I mean even in the pandemic  where we're you know we know we got to  make policy this week next week probably  every week for the next six months I  still think lifting your head up a bit  and thinking about the long-term  implications is incredibly important and  if you get too sucked into the  day-to-day policy you often kind of  start to lose sight of the bigger  picture you know where is it we're going  why are we going and I think that's a  very valuable asset that academics  probably sometimes underestimate now  admittedly you can't say I'll come back  in 20 years and give you an answer but I  think still being able to do slightly  longer-term thinking is really very very  important absolutely critical and in a  kind of great spot for us to conclude  today's discussion on thank you so much  Tim that's all the time we have for  today and so before everyone signs off  I'd like to thank all of you for joining  us and I'd especially like to thank you  Tim for your insights today dreamily  extremely interesting and useful  conversation thank you so much I'd also  like to thank Rachel orchard Parker for  her inspiration and organization of  today's talk as well as Daniel patch  nello and Jacqui Sullivan in the  proverbial control room please visit