 can  you talk a little bit about that and  what you think may have been some of  some of the impacts of of the pandemic  on the trust in the different  institutions we have in our societies  yeah and let me I think people often say  Trust is hard earned and easily lost as  well and I think that often reflects  some of what we experience but I think  in the pandemic it's been particularly  important the extent of reliance we have  on compliance with various measures that  have been taken and how much trust we  place in in the wider populous to follow  what might be called sensible advice  rather than thinking everything has to  be solved through rules and regulations  so it's kind of getting that balance  right between what you trust citizens to  do and what you trust government to do  is absolutely central and I think what  I've been struck by both with my  experience in the pandemic but also  reflecting on other countries is how the  institutional framework tries to get  that balance right and there are a  number of dimensions I think that are  worth remarking on one is the kind of  transparency of the advice I mean where  is it coming from  on what basis has it been formed is the  evidence base as solid as some people  would claim and it's and it's important  to be - I think be straight with people  about that not to over claim what  evidence can show particularly in areas  where the evidence is less than  clear-cut and you build trust I think  with a certain degree of humility and  honesty but you can't  in the end throw your arms up and say  well we don't know anything that's not  helpful either it's kind of trying to  get what I will regard as a reasonable  default position a position you can  defend short of evidence moving you away  from it and I think the art of it and  you can get to this in different ways is  is to hit upon a default option that is  supportable by evidence and you and you  don't feel compelled to keep shifting  ground because if you go to the wrong  place and evidence is repeatedly coming  up to say you're in the wrong place it  will undermine trust again in the whole  system not just in that specific piece  of advice and so as much as possible you  try and get those default positions  right as quickly and early as possible a  little bit about what we've seen over  the last several months with Koba 19 is  that there's quite a dynamic nature to  that interplay as the evidence changes  when the health advice evolves and we  see that tension between providing  science advice and the role of the  politician and the institutions of  government so there's a really  interesting dynamic around this rapid  response mechanism and I wanted to talk  to you a little bit about Dell which is  a group that's been put together that  you're a member of in the UK led by the  Royal Society  I think Dell stands for data evaluation  and learning for viral epidemics if I  look at my notes and it's really trying  to apply data evaluation and system and  synthesis to different approaches that  have been taken and it's an  interdisciplinary group as well but  quite rapidly developing this this  knowledge and information into  government and the actions that move  forward can you talk a little bit about  how that has worked and what you think  some of the benefits of that might have  been and maybe potentially some of the  risks yeah so I mean many ways delve is  a reflection of the what safar's mantra  is because it's trying to be issue  driven and bring expertise regardless of  discipline together based around around  issues so I felt quite at home being a  part of that even though there's an  economist I'm in a small minority that  the disciplines the many disciplines  involved epidemiology Public Health  Medical Sciences of different kinds so  it's been a very interesting experience  trying to bring a group  people together to work on problems and  to produce reports which we hope will be  useful to informing policy an example  would be the report we did on testing  tracing and isolation another one on the  use of face masks as other reports in in  process so trying to sort of look at the  different approaches disciplines take to  evidence what kind of evidence they  value let me give you an example I don't  think in a pandemic like this you can  put too much weight on randomized  control trials not because they're not  useful when you can have them but many  of the pieces of advice you're giving  simply don't have an RCT evidence base  so if you said well we're only going to  do those things that are proven by  enemy's control trials we're certainly  not going to get very far when the  degree of uncertainty and lack of  appropriate knowledge exists so it's  kind of then working together and saying  well what evidence is persuasive and and  also getting a wider a wider perspective  on on on the evidence to what extent we  want to bring the economic cost in  alongside some of the the costs to  infection you know there's been this  balancing act which I'm sure you're  familiar with in Canada between trying  to get the public health right and  trying to get the economics right  without without compromising either too  much and and again through conversations  within within a multidisciplinary  environment we can we can sort of start  to speak each other's language or  understand each other's language but  ultimately the view is we need to put  out there the best of thinking that we  can find and and then input that into  the policy process yeah it's it's very  helpful I think I think the interesting  approach to to look out to different  jurisdictions so I often think about  this is one of the things that CFR finds  really valuable in our process is having  research programs that are made up of  senior academic leaders who are based in  different institutions and different  disciplines and in different countries  being able to bring that together we  were able to hold an international  consultation with several  individuals based in the UK as part of  the work we did on the society  technology and ethics report as well how  do you find that reaching into your  international networks understanding  what's happening outside of the UK to  then be able to provide advice into the  UK government well I think I mean I  think goes goes without saying I'm sure  you know that we're we are all academics  typically embedded in international  networks we're all that frustrated that  we're house bound now and can't be  participating in international  conferences I think zoom has provided a  platform at least for us to could stay  in start in close communication with our  academic colleagues around the world  because most of our disciplines are  highly internationalized and therefore  we are all the time trying to look and  see where the best the best knowledge is  even though you know you working in a  country context as we are I mean we're  trying to feed into the UK policy  response many much of the time it's what  have we learned from country X or Y  about how they they approached it and  can we feed that in one of the experts  saying why are they saying eventually  something different you know is there a  justifiable reason for taking a  different position does it just reflect  the cultural or institutions of another  country so when it's constantly trying  to synthesize and understand there's  different perspectives to try and kind  of work out what to conclude from that  it's more I mean I slightly speaking  against the title of the seminar it's  more of an art than a science I would  say being able to sift the evidence and  get that right but at the end of the day  the science is a big input into the art  if you like fair enough that makes it  that makes a lot of sense so one area  that you and I have just touched on  briefly was around the world of the  private sector so there are many actors  in an economy all of them have different  roles can you talk a little bit about  the role that you see of the firms and  the private sector as part of the  pandemic response so we think about  public health and the  cows response we think about economic  stimulus and innovation as a form of  recovery out of the pandemic but what  have you seen in terms of the dynamics  around the private sector engaging with  the government or being part of the  public health response there's a couple  of things on that I'd like to say what  one is the sort of direct involvement in  the private sector trying to deliver a  kind of recovery phase there is little  doubt that we will have to get the  private sector back into some kind of  decent shape to provide the jobs and  livelihoods for people who've been  affected by the pandemic but equally  there will be a different world out  there one where business as usual will  not be possible in many sectors of the  economy at least for the foreseeable  future - we hopefully get a vaccine or a  more effective treatment and so it's a  case of trying to work with with  business to to make the best of what is  a difficult situation for example what  kind of testing protocols does one want  in place how does one encourage them are  they a matter of voluntary compliance or  compulsion I think we all think that in  the end trying to get people to do  things voluntarily is advantageous but  that's not always feasible so you have  to decide where there's the limelight  regulation and volunteerism  but many firms that particularly those  that want to get back to normal business  have stepped up and started voluntarily  to engage in public health measures and  not an entirely frivolous example is the  professional soccer to you football to  us leagues which have basically in order  to be able to be allowed to play again  have put in very rigorous testing  regimes for players and staff because  they realized that was the only route  back to some kind of normal operation of  their business even though in fact all  the fixtures have played behind closed  doors so that's one aspect the other  aspect which I mentioned briefly is the  provision of data so you know we we've  been very clear in this pandemic how  much have one of wealth of intelligence  and information resides with the private  sector whether they be mobile phone  companies that  because to put up tracing apps or  whatever through financial firms that  can have a much more up-to-date and  up-to-the-minute picture of the impact  of the of the pandemic on the economy so  one needs to form sort of public-private  partnerships to both access and analyze  data now raises quite a few issues not  least around data privacy and so it's  not it's not altogether straightforward  to know how you how you do that but it  has been I think an interesting feature  of the pandemic that need to forge those  strong partnerships and to together to  try and do the best and I think some of  this will outlive the the the pandemic  that new ways of looking at the economy  through the lens of the data that the  private sector has or looking at public  health measures through the same lens  will become something we tweet we may  say in the end you know we want we want  to maintain this this is this is  important for creating more effective  intervention and better societies that's  a really interesting point I think we've  certainly seen in Canada that the wealth  of data that we have within our health  systems many of them at the provincial  level really a really renewed  understanding of the value of the data  and the capacity for it to inform  research and decision making you know  both in terms of when it when it doesn't  work well and then you know in terms of  Public Health surveillance and otherwise  but then also when you know really the  benefit in thinking about how do we  sustain and support those data  infrastructures in a way that will allow  the research and work that needs to be  done it's a whole other question when  you think about the private sector and  as you rightly admit you have all sorts  of constraints consent based privacy  policies and otherwise that exists  that data so what does that mean in  terms of thinking about how to integrate  that or not  into government systems as well and I  think that will be one of I I think that  is going to be one of the legacies that  will come out of this time will be some  understanding about what data and what  systems we need to have within our  government structures and then also and  will what the role of the private sector  might be in that regard I want to we've  got one question from from the audience  that I want to use drills in to the  point you made earlier Tim around around  public trust and transparency and the  question is really that oftentimes a  politician will say that their their  advice is based on science but they may  not in fact indicate where they got the  scientific advice from our what the  evidence might be to support that advice  so how does the way in which a  politician communicates about the  science and the evidence base for their  information how does that affect the  trust that citizens have in that  information and in that advice I think  this has been is a very interesting  question because this has been I think  one of the major areas where we be  learning as we go  you know how you through through the  pandemic in many countries people who  are scientist I anticipated it become  sort of public figures because  scientists so because politicians in  particular have found it convenient to  put them on pedestals and to have them  directly communicate with the public in  order for them to try and answer  questions concerns that the public have  so so sometimes instead of the  politician being the mouthpiece for this  is the advice they you know if you if  you ever tuned in to the broadcast we  have in the UK the press conferences  you're very often finding the politician  will hand off the question directly to a  scientist and say this is one for the  scientist now please answer them there's  no the scientists can't hide at that  point because they either state an  opinion or the  so then you can't just say a scientist  believes you really you know figure out  who's who's saying what but I think  there's a sort of wider background  concern and this is why it's been  evolving through the pandemic because  initially the main scientific body in  the UK Sage there was not even any  transparency about who sat on that body  and then at one point they were forced  to publish the names and and as time  goes by people are asking for even more  information maybe they should publish  the minutes for those of us who used to  work in central banking will be familiar  with this I used to publish our central  bank minutes after every policy decision  to communicate to the public why we were  reaching a decision but now now that may  become a feature of these more  institutionalized scientific bodies that  they're they have to publish minutes and  then maybe they also have to publish  some of the sources for that those  minutes which particular studies because  as we know it's rarely that you have  just a single study that's going to  convince you to shift policy but if  that's really what shifter policy people  should be honest about that what was the  evidence that was decisive and so this  is a this has been a very interesting  area to watch evolving countries will  learn from each other they'll look at  what systems are in place and decide  whether they want the more the system in  which the politicians have put more on  the pedestal less on the pedestal quoted  not quoted but but I think because this  is all hitters in such a short time  we're very much learning on on the job  as far as that goes  absolutely so really interesting  question around the politicization of  science of bodies so we've seen for  example in the u.s. around face masks  and face masks where in that there's  that's become a rather partisan issue so  how you know what advice would you give  to scientists who were providing policy  advice  how does want to stay away from or to  avoid the politicization of some of the  advice they're providing well the sort  of one level of politicization that I  think is in the hands of the individuals  concerned and that's the extent to which  they want to speak out or not against  what is palpably government policy I  think once you do that you've kind of  crossed the line and you have in whether  you like it or not become part of the  political process and I think that's  always been an issue that scientists or  economists or anyone else face once you  once you become oppositional you do  become part of the political process but  I think that the way kind of more  generally I think about this is to come  back to this idea of a default policy  that I think there's sort of two stances  you can take one is that this policy is  contrary is contravening of view of what  the evidence says is a good policy  that's a very strong statement versus  saying well I can't provide compelling  evidence to move from this policy I'm  not sure it's right or wrong but so I  think there's a sort of difference  between saying we know this is wrong  versus we don't have sufficient evidence  to move away from it and I think it's  when you cross that line into yes I know  this is wrong and you're willing to  state so publicly that you become as  they say part of the political process  but very often science or economics or  anything else you study it's not so  clear-cut that you can denounce  something as evidently a bad policy so a  good example is the face mask I'll just  mention this briefly because I think  early on in the pandemic the view should  probably have been taken that face masks  wearing face masks in confined spaces  was was the right policy that wasn't the  policy we took in the UK I don't know  for sure what you what you decided in  Canada now it's not because I could  prove or anyone else could prove that  wearing a face mask is such a great idea  I mean you can certainly hard to believe  it's a bad idea but it's certainly not  evidently proven that it's a good idea  so that shouldn't become the default  policy that once you take the default  policy to be you should wear face.you so  you shouldn't wear  masks you shift about burden of proof  and and that's a very different  proposition which again I think is very  hard to prove so so I think making those  choices about where you think the  presumption lies can make a lot of  difference to the way the public debate  evolved okay we have just a few minutes  left but I really I just would love you  to take a moment because clearly  providing policy advice has been a part  of your academic career right from the  very earliest days you've been involved  in many different forms of providing  policy advice and I wondered if you  could just sort of reflect on that I  think there'd be a lot of researchers on  the call on this talk who would be  interested to hear why was that  important to your academic career and if  there was an experience that was  particularly rewarding what was it so I  think the time it dawned on me more than  any other time was when I was a  policymaker you mentioned I was in  Central Bank during the financial crisis  and of course there I was an effective  policymaker taking part in the response  to that crisis only in a narrow sense of  the monetary policy at the time but a  couple of things occurred to me that  reflect on your question one was that as  a someone whose that was three years of  my life or all the rest of my life I've  been essentially in academic but what  was interesting about that experience in  terms of my academic life was realizing  that there's two things that I think  academics can really contribute and  that's often to provide a framework for  thinking about the policy issues which  well it won't necessarily resolve the  issue because the issue is itself very  complex you can by Framing in a  particular way make transparent to those  who have to make decisions kind of what  the pros and cons are broadly of taking  different stances and where the evidence  would definitely say you shouldn't do  something versus where it doesn't prove  you shouldn't do something but it might  be prudent to do it so I think one of  the things that I've always found  rewarding if you like engagement in  policy is where you think you can you  can make a contribution by framing an  issue in a in a in a particular in a  particular way so so I think that's  that's a valuable part of what we do and  link to that academics can take a long  term view I mean even in the pandemic  where we're you know we know we got to  make policy this week next week probably  every week for the next six months I  still think lifting your head up a bit  and thinking about the long-term  implications is incredibly important and  if you get too sucked into the  day-to-day policy you often kind of  start to lose sight of the bigger  picture you know where is it we're going  why are we going and I think that's a  very valuable asset that academics  probably sometimes underestimate now  admittedly you can't say I'll come back  in 20 years and give you an answer but I  think still being able to do slightly  longer-term thinking is really very very  important absolutely critical and in a  kind of great spot for us to conclude  today's discussion on thank you so much  Tim that's all the time we have for  today and so before everyone signs off  I'd like to thank all of you for joining  us and I'd especially like to thank you  Tim for your insights today dreamily  extremely interesting and useful  conversation thank you so much I'd also  like to thank Rachel orchard Parker for  her inspiration and organization of  today's talk as well as Daniel patch  nello and Jacqui Sullivan in the  proverbial control room please visit our  website see far CA to find out more  about the series interview a recording  of this talk along with previously  recorded ones thank you all for joining  us and goodbye  you
 

